Metal-­‐organic	   polyhedral	   (MOP)	   is	   a	   newly-­‐developed class	   of	   inorganic-­‐organic	   discrete	   coordination	   complexes, which	  is	 usually	  prepared	  by	  self-­‐assembly	  of	  metal	  ions	  and highly	   directional	   and	   functionalized	   ligands. 	   Due	   to	   the tailored	  structure	  and	  special	  symmetry,	  MOP	  experiences	  a rapid	   development	   in	   recent	   years	   and	   has	   exhibited	   a	   wide range	   of	   applications	   such	   as	   molecular	   flasks	   or	   molecular recognizers.2a-­‐c	  As	  one	  of	  the	  most	  representative	  MOP, $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ features	  an	  octahedral	  self-­‐assembly	  (Scheme	  1a).1l,3	  Although this	  MOP	  has	  some	  unique	  advantages	  such	  as	  highly	  watersoluble	   and	   commercially	   available,	   its	   application	   is	   limited to	   molecular	   sensing	   or	   photocatalysis	   under	   mild	   condi-­‐ tions.2a-­‐c,4	  This	  limitation	  is	  mostly	  associated	  with	  the	  insta-­‐ bility	   of	   MOPs,	   which	   tend	   to	   aggregate	   under	   relatively harsh	   reaction	   conditions	   (such	   as	   high	   temperatures).2d,e Encapsulation	  of	  MOP	  into	  the	  cavity	  of	  a	  host	  with	  windows smaller	   than	   the	   MOP	   could	   circumvent	   this	   problem,	   and thus	  greatly	  broaden	  the	  application	  scope	  of	  MOPs.
Metal-­‐organic	   frameworks	   (MOFs)	   are	   a	   new	   class	   of	   porous	  materials. 	  Particularly,	  the	  well-­‐defined	  and	  functionalizable	   pores	   may	   provide	   accommodative	   spaces,	   making MOFs	   one	   type	   of	   the	   most	   promising	   candidates	   for	   the encapsulation	   of	   various	   guests	   (e.g.,	   metal	   nanoparticles, organometallic	  complexes,	  and	  polyoxometallate).6	  The	  great structural	   and	   chemical	   tunability	   of	   MOFs	   have	   led	   to	   a variety	   of	   methodologies	   for	   the	   preparation	   of	   such host−guest	  composites	  by	  incorporating	  guest	  molecule	  as	  an entity,	   such	   as	   impregnation,7a,b	   chemical	   vapor	   deposi-­‐ tion,7c, d	   and	   cation	   exchange,7e	   endowing	   the	   composites with	  excellent	  performances	  in	  catalysis,	  gas	  adsorption,	  etc.8
Despite	   the	   success,	   these	   methods	   are	   usually	   restricted	   by the	   MOF	   aperture	   size,	   resulting	   in	   inaccessibility	   for	   larger guest	   molecules.	   Coassembly	   of	   the	   guest	   during	   MOF	   crystal	   growth	   offers	   a	   solution	   to	   this	   problem.	   However,	   it	   often	  perturbs	  MOF	  growth.	  Recently,	  Ma’s	  group	  developed	  a new	   strategy	   for	   guest	   encapsulation	   into	   MOF	   via	   metal-­‐ cation-­‐directed	   de	   novo	   assembly	   from	   the	   component	   fragments	   of	   the	   guest	   molecule.9	   More	   recently,	   Tsung’s	   group introduced	  a	  new	  concept	  for	  incorporating	  large	  guest	  mol-­‐ ecules	  as	  entity	  into	  MOFs	  via	  expanded	  apertures	  created	  by dissociative	   linker	   exchange.10	   These	   strategies	   might	   offer new	   insights	   into	   such	   host−guest	   system	   and	   impart	   new interests	   in	   developing	   more	   general	   and	   facile	   approaches for	  preparation	  of	  the	  type	  of	  composites.
Herein,	  we	  report	  a	  novel	  hydrophilicity-­‐directed	  approach (HDA),	   based	   on	   the	   different	   hydrophilicities	   of	   the	   guest and	   host,	   for	   the	   encapsulation	   of	   MOP	   larger	   than	   MOF aperture	  size	  into	  the	  MOF	  pores.	  The	  HDA	  strategy	  typically includes	  two	  steps:	  (1)	  introducing	  a	  small	  amount	  of	  solvent (I)	   suitable	   for	   MOP	   assembly	   into	   the	   pre-­‐formed	   MOF	   using	   a	   two-­‐solvent	   system and	   (2)	   assembling	   the	   metal	   salts and	  ligands	  that	  are	  introduced	  into	  the	  MOF	  pores	  by	  dissolution	  or	  diffusion	  to	  form	  the	  MOP.
As	   a	   proof	   of	   principle,	   we	   employed	   MIL-­‐101(Cr)	   as	   the host	  and $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ as	  a	  model	  MOP	  guest,	  respectively.	  The	  MIL-­‐ $\mathrm{{\bf{\tau}}_{\mathrm{{1o1}}}(C r)}$ ,	  a	  representative	  MOF,	  holds	  two	  types	  of	  mesopores with	  internal	  free	  diameters	  of	  ca.	  29	  Å	  and $\bar{34}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ .11	  The	  smaller	  one	  possesses	  pentagonal	  windows	  with	  a	  free	  opening	  of \~12	  Å,	  while	  the	  larger	  one	  owns	  both	  pentagonal	  and	  hexagonal	  windows	  with	  an	  aperture	  of $14{\cdot}5\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and	  16	  Å,	  respectively. $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ is	  a	  hollow	  octahedral	  self-­‐assembly	  with	  a	  diagonal Pd−Pd	   distance	   of	   2.2	   nm,	   in	   which	   M	   stands	   for $\mathrm{(en)Pd(NO_{3})_{2}}$ and	   L	   stands	   for	   1,3,5-­‐Tris $\scriptstyle4$ -­‐pyridyl)- $^{\cdot2,4,6-}$ triazine	   (TPT).3	   In	   this	   complex,	   the	   six	   vertices	   of	   the	   octahedron	  are	  occupied	  by	  cationic	  M,	  whereas	  the	  eight	  triangular	   faces	   are	   alternately	   occupied	   by	   four	   exo-­‐tridentate triangular	   ligands	   L	   (Scheme	   1a). 	   As	   the	   pore	   windows	   of MIL-­‐101(Cr)	  are	  smaller	  than $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}.$ ,	   the	   migration	   and	   leach-­‐ ing	  of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ could	  be	  effectively	  prohibited	  if	  it	  is	  encapsulated	  in	  the	  MOF	  pore.
The	   synthesis	   process	   by	   using	   the	   HDA	   strategy	   is	   illustrated	   in	   Scheme	   1b.	   Typically,	   activated	   MIL-­‐101	   and	   L	   were first	  added	  into	  a	  large	  amount	  of	  n-­‐hexane,	  and	  then	  a	  small amount	   of	   aqueous	   M	   solution $(V_{\mathrm{solution}}<V_{\mathrm{pore~of~MOl}}$ F)	   was added.	   As	   MIL-­‐101	   inner	   surface	   was	   more	   hydrophilic	   than the	   outer	   surface,	   the	   small	   amount	   of	   aqueous $\mathbf{M}$ solution was	   readily	   incorporated	   into	   the	   MIL-­‐101	   pores	   by	   capillary force.12	  Thus,	  MIL-­‐101	  was	  suspended	  in	  n-­‐hexane	  with	  pores filled	  with	  aqueous $\mathbf{M}$ solution.	  Because $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ was	  easily	  selfassembled	  in	  water, $^3\mathrm{~L~}$ was	  drawn	  into	  the	  MIL-­‐101	  pores	  due to	  the	  chemical	  equilibrium	  shift	  of	  the	  self-­‐assembly	  process of	  forming $\mathbf{M}_{6}\mathbf{L}_{4}$ .
The	   obtained	   materials	   were	   subsequently	   washed	   with	   a large	  quantity	  of	  deionized	  water	  until	  no	  Pd	  traces	  could	  be detected	   in	   the	   filtrate.	   This	   operation	   was	   performed	   carefully	   in	   order	   to	   exclude	   the	   influence	   of	   the	   absorbed	   Pd species	   on	   the	   MOF	   surface.	   Finally,	   the	   hybrids	   were	   acti-­‐ vated	   by	   heating	   at $\mathbf{150}\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for	   12	   h	   under	   dynamic	   vacuum. The	   as-­‐synthesized	   materials	   are	   denoted	   as $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{\subsetMIL}$ -­‐101- N $(\Nu=3,4,6$ ,	  or	  10),	  where $\mathsf{N}$ represents	  the	  average	  number of	   MOF	   cavities	   that	   accommodate	   one $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ in	   theory,	   according	   to	   the	   amount	   of	   the	   precursors	   added	   in	   the	   reaction	   solution.	   The	   actual	   Pd	   contents	   doped	   in	   MOF	   were measured	   by	   Atomic	   Absorption	   Spectroscopy	   (AAS),	   showing	  a	  high	  doping	  efficiency	  (ca. $90\%$ )	  of	  Pd	  (Table	  S1).
The	  crystallinity	  of	  the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{\subsetMIL}$ -­‐101	  materials	  was	  examined	  by	  powder	  X-­‐ray	  diffraction	  (PXRD).	  As	  shown	  in	  Figure S1,	  the	  PXRD	  patterns	  of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{\subsetMIL}$ -­‐101	  hybrids	  were	  similar to	   the	   parent	   MIL-­‐101,11	   demonstrating	   the	   well	   preservation of	  crystalline	  structure	  during	  the	  incorporation	  process.
The	   successful	   incorporation	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ on	   MOF	   was	   con-­‐ firmed	   by	   NMR	   spectroscopy	   taking $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{101}{-}4$ as	   an example.	   The	   liquid $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR	   spectrum	   of	   pure $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ was shown	   in	   Fig.	   S2,	   which	   was	   consistent	   with	   the	   literature report.3	   However,	   the	   solid-­‐state	   NMR	   spectra	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ have never	   been	   reported.	   In	   this	   work,	   the $^{13}{\cal C}$ solid-­‐state	   NMR spectrum	  of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ (Fig.	  1a	  and	  Fig. $S_{3}$ )	  showed	  principally	  five peaks.	  The	  signals	  at $\updelta=168$ .99	  ppm,	  153.26	  ppm,	  143.57	  ppm, and	   124.90	   ppm	   were	   characteristic	   peaks	   of	   L,	   while	   the peak	  located	  at $\updelta=48.65$ ppm	  corresponded	  to	  the	  ethanedi-­‐ amine	  group	  in	  M.	  In	  contrast,	  the	  parent	  MIL-­‐101	  showed	  no obvious	   NMR	   signal	   because	   MIL-­‐101	   contains	   paramagnetic Cr	   centers.	   The	   strong	   dipolar	   interactions	   between	   the	   un-­‐ paired	   electrons	   of	   the	   paramagnetic $C\mathbf{r}$ centers	   and	   the NMR-­‐active	   nuclei	   accelerate	   nuclear	   spin	   relaxation	   and shift	   NMR	   signals,	   making	   some	   signals	   “invisible”. 	   As	   ex-­‐ pected,	  the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL-101^{-}}4$ (Fig.	  1a)	  also	  showed	  the	  characteristic	   peaks	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ .	   The	   peaks	   intensity	   was	   much	   lower than	   the	   pristine $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ due	   to	   the	   low	   content	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ confined	   in	   the	   hybrid.	   These	   results	   demonstrated	   that $\mathbf{M}_{6}\mathbf{L}_{4}$ was	   successfully	   synthesized	   and	   doped	   on	   the	   MIL-­‐101.	   Ad-­‐ ditionally,	  no	  obvious	  chemical	  shifts	  were	  observed,	  indicating	   the	   structural	   and	   chemical	   properties	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ were	   not perturbed	  during	  the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{\subsetMIL}$ -­‐101	  synthesis	  process.
The	   incorporation	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ on	   MIL-­‐101	   was	   further	   confirmed	   by	   Fourier	   Transform	   infrared	   spectroscopy	   (FT-­‐IR). As	   presented	   in	   Fig.	   1b,	   the	   spectrum	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{101}\mathrm{-}4$ exhibited	   characteristic	   bonds	   of	   both $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ and	   MIL-­‐101. Noteworthy,	   the	   peaks	   at	   1061 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were	   assigned	   to	   C-­‐N stretching	   in	   the	   L.	   No	   obvious	   shifts	   were	   also	   observed between	   the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL-101^{-}}4$ and	   pristine $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4},$ indicating that	   the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ in	   MIL-­‐101	   pores	   preserved	   its	   original	   struc-­‐ ture	   and	   property.	   The	   characteristic	   framework	   -­‐(O-­‐C-­‐O)- band	   at	   ca.	   1400 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was	   broadened	   obviously	   and	   slightly shifted	   after $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ incorporation,	   suggesting	   possible	   encapsulation	  of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ in	  the	  MIL-­‐101	  pores.
TEM,	  high-­‐angle	  annular	  dark-­‐field	  scanning	  transmission electron	   microscopy	   (HAADF-­‐STEM),	   and	   energy	   dispersive X-­‐ray	   spectroscopy	   (EDS)	   elemental	   mapping	   analyses	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL}\mathrm{-}101{-}4$ showed	   no	   distinctly	   congregated	   Pd	   parti-­‐ cles,	   demonstrating	   a	   uniform	   distribution	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ in	   the MOF	   (Fig.	   S4a	   and	   Fig.	   S5a-­‐c).	   The	   HAADF-­‐STEM	   and	   elemental	   mapping	   images	   (Fig.	   2	   and	   Fig.	   S6)	   of	   the	   ultrathin
