slices	   from $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{101}{-}4$ confirmed	   that	   Cr	   and	   Pd	   elements	   were	   distributed	   homogenously,	   indicating	   that $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ was	   uniformly	   dispersed	   in	   the	   MIL-­‐101	   cavities.	   Moreover, scanning	   electron	   microscopy	   (SEM)	   and	   EDS	   elemental mapping	  analyses	  (Fig.	  S7)	  also	  revealed	  an	  even	  distribution of	  Cr,	  Pd,	  and	  N	  all	  over	  the	  octahedral	  crystals.	  These	  measurements	   indicated	   that	   the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ was	   homogeneously	   distributed	  in	  the	  MIL-­‐101	  pores	  without	  the	  presence	  of	  neither accumulation	  in	  a	  particular	  region	  nor	  clusters	  of	  bulk $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ that	  were	  physically	  mixed	  with	  the	  parent	  MOF.
To	  investigate	  the	  potential	  interactions	  between $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ and MIL-­‐101,	  we	  further	  characterized	  the	  materials	  by	  X-­‐ray	  photoelectron	  spectroscopy	  (XPS).	  N	  and	  Pd	  elements	  were	  presented	  obviously	  in	  the	  composites	  as	  shown	  by	  the	  XPS	  survey	  spectrum	  of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{101}{-}4$ (Fig. $3^{\mathrm{c}}$ and	  Fig.	  S8).	  The	  Pd XPS	   spectra	   of	   both $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{\subsetMIL}$ -­‐101- $^{\cdot}4$ (Fig.	   3b)	   exhibited	   Pd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ bands	   at	   ca. $338.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}_{3}\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ bands	   at ca.	   343.6	   eV,	   typical	   characters	   of	   Pd	   (II)	   ions. $^{14}\mathrm{No}$ apprecia-­‐ ble	   differences	   were	   observed	   in	   the	   binding	   energies	   of	   the ${\mathrm{Pd}}({\mathrm{3d}})$ spectra	   for	   the	   two	   materials.	   Similarly,	   there	   was	   no apparent	   shift	   between	   the	   Cr	   binding	   energies	   for	   the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL-101-}4$ and	   MIL-­‐101	   (Fig.	   3a).	   These	   observations suggested	   that	   no	   strong	   electron	   interaction	   between	   the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ and  MIL-­‐101	  was	  present	  in	  the	  composites.
The	  surface	  areas	  and	  porosities	  of	  the	  hybrids	  were	  measured	   by $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption	   at $_{77}\mathrm{~K~}$ .	   The $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{\subsetMIL}$ -­‐101	   samples (Fig.	   S9)	   all	   showed	   type	   I	   isotherms	   similar	   to	   that	   of	   the parent	   MIL-­‐101,	   indicating	   that	   the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{\subsetMIL}$ -­‐101	   materials still	  maintained	  the	  porous	  structure	  of	  MIL-­‐101. In	  comparison	   with	   the	   parent	   MIL-­‐101,	   obvious	   decreases	   in	   the	   BET surface	  areas	  and	  pore	  volumes	  (Table	  S2)	  were	  observed	  due to	  the	  occupation	  of	  the	  pores,	  further	  confirming	  the	  incorporation	  of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ within	  the	  MIL-­‐101	  pores.9,10	  As	  the	  content of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ increased,	  the	  BET	  surface	  areas	  and	  pore	  volumes	  of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{\subsetMIL}$ -­‐101	  decreased	  gradually	  as	  expected.
To	  elucidate	  the	  advantages	  of	  our	  HDA	  assembly	  strategy for	   the	   encapsulation	   of	   MOP	   in	   MOFs,	   two	   traditional methods	   were	   also	   attempted	   for	   the	   incorporation	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ into	   MIL-­‐101.	   The	   obtained	   materials	   are	   named	   as $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}_{6}L_{4}/M L}$ -­‐101	  and $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}$ -­‐101,	  respectively.	  For	  the	  preparation	   of $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}_{6}L_{4}/M L}$ -­‐101	   by	   impregnation,	   MIL-­‐101	   particles were	   directly	   soaked	   in	   the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ aqueous	   solution.	   The	   impregnation	  approach	  was	  not	  efficient	  for $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ incorporation as	   evidenced	   by	   the	   extremely	   low $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ loading	   (Table	   S1 and	  Figs.	  S10-­‐S11).	  Moreover,	  HAADF-­‐STEM	  images	  (Fig.	  S12a) showed	   that $\mathbf{M}_{6}\mathbf{L}_{4}$ was	   aggregated	   on	   the	   MOF	   surface.	   The attempt	   to	   encapsulate $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ into	   MIL-­‐101	   employing	   only one	   solvent,	   i.e.,	   soaking	   MIL-­‐101	   in	   the	   M	   and	   L	   mixed aqueous	   solution,	   did	   not	   succeed	   either	   (Table $\mathrm{S1}$ and	   Figs. $\mathrm{S}_{13}–\mathrm{S}_{14}\rangle$ ).	   Additionally,	   leaching	   experiments	   showed	   that $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}_{6}L_{4}/M L}$ -­‐101	   and $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}$ -­‐101	   underwent	   obvious	   Pd leaching	  while $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{\subsetMIL}$ -­‐101	  didn’t	  show	  any	  significant	  loss of	   Pd	   (Table $S_{3}$ ).	   These	   comparisons	   highlighted	   the	   effectiveness	   of	   the	   HDA	   strategy	   for	   encapsulating	   large	   MOP molecules	  into	  the	  pores	  of	  MOFs.
To	   demonstrate	   that	   the	   HDA	   strategy	   can	   enhance	   the stability	   and	   performance	   of	   MOP	   confined	   in	   MOF,	   we	   examined	   the	   catalytic	   property	   of	   the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{\subsetMIL}$ -­‐101	   hybrids in	   the	   selective	   oxidation	   of	   benzyl	   alcohol	  to	   benzaldehyde (Fig.	   4	   and	   Table $S_{4}$ ).	  The	  blank	  reactions	  without	  a	  catalyst or	  even	  with	  MIL-­‐101	  gave	  essentially	  no	  conversions $(<5\%)$ at 130 $^{\circ}C$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ exhibited	   some	   reactivity	   and	   afforded $60\%$ conversion	  with $45\%$ selectivity	  to	  benzaldehyde	  after	  18 h	  of	  reaction	  (Fig.	  4),	  with	  benzoic	  acid	  and	  benzyl	  benzoate as	   the	   main	   side-­‐products.	   In	   comparison,	   the $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{\mathbf{\subsetMIL}}.$ - 101-­‐4	   showed	   significantly	   improved	   activity	   and	   selectivity, giving $98\%$ selectivity	   to	   benzaldehyde	   at $95\%$ conversion under	  identical	  conditions.	  As	  compared	  to $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4},$ it	   was	   impressive	  that	  the	  benzaldehyde	  yield	  was	  enhanced	  by	  a	  factor	   of	   ca.	   3.5.	   More	   importantly,	   the	   catalytic	   activity	   and selectivity	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{101}{-}4$ remained	   almost	   unchanged even	   after	   five	   reuses,	   while	   the	   pristine $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ deactivated dramatically	   (Fig.	   4).	   As	   a	   consequence,	   the	   benzaldehyde yield	   obtained	   on	   the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{101}{-}4$ system	   was	   nearly	   20 times	   higher	   than	   that	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ after	   being	   reused	   for	   five times.	   Both $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}$ -­‐101	   and $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}_{6}L_{4}/M L}$ -­‐101	   showed	   much inferior	  catalytic	  performance	  compared	  to $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL-101^{-}}4$ . A	  possible	  reaction	  mechanism	  was	  proposed	  in	  Scheme	  S1.
The	   stability	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{101}{-}4$ was	   confirmed	   by	   TEM and	  HAADF-­‐STEM	  measurements,	  which	  showed	  no	  obvious catalyst	   aggregation	   after	   recycling	   (Fig.	   S4b	   and	   Fig.	   S5d-­‐i).
XPS	   measurements	   of	   the	   recovered $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{101}{-}4$ indicated	  that	  palladium	  remained	  in	  its	  divalent	  form	  (Fig.	  S15). The	   well	   preservation	   of	   the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{101}{-}4$ structure	   after reactions	  was	  further	  demonstrated	  by	  FT-­‐IR	  analysis,	  which showed	  almost	  identical	  spectrum	  as	  the	  fresh	  one	  (Fig.	  S16). However,	  the $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ system	  encountered	  serious	  catalyst	  degradation	  during	  reaction,	  in	  which	  palladium	  aggregates	  with diameters	   of $5^{-20\ \mathrm{nm}}$ were	   clearly	   observed	   even	   only	   after the	   first	   run	   (Fig.	   S4d).	   The	   remarkably	   enhanced	   reactivity and	   stability	   of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{CMIL}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{101}{-}4$ as	   compared	   to $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ is	   believed	  to	  be	  related	  to	  the	  encapsulation	  effect	  offered	  by	  the MOF	  cavities,	  featuring	  highly	  dispersed $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ active	  centers without	  any	  significant	  leaching	  or	  aggregation.
In	   summary,	   we	   have	   developed,	   for	   the	   first	   time,	   a	   hydrophilicity-­‐directed	   approach	   (HDA)	   for	   the	   encapsulation of	  MOP	  into	  MOFs	  beyond	  the	  framework	  aperture	  size	  limitation,	  as	  exemplified	  by	  the	  self-­‐assembly	  of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ into	  MIL101.	  The	  confined $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ exhibited	  significantly	  improved	  cata-­‐ lytic	  efficiency	  and	  stability	  compared	  with	  the	  pristine	  MOP material.	  The	  HDA	  strategy	  requires	  neither	  particular	  types of	  MOFs	  nor	  destruction	  of	  the	  MOFs,	  thus	  offers	  a	  versatile approach	   to	   encapsulate	   a	   broad	   range	   of	   MOP	   complexes into	   MOFs	   with	   significantly	   enhanced	   performances	   for various	  applications.	  Studies	  aimed	  at	  extending	  this	  strategy to	   the	   encapsulation	   of	   other	   types	   of	   MOPs	   into	   MOFs	   for advanced	   catalysis	   applications	   are	   currently	   underway	   in our	  laboratory.
